ERNOBUILDS vmalloc lock repair (unofficial)
============================================

Status, attribution, and support
--------------------------------

This branch is an unofficial ERNOBUILDS modification of Glade Kernel. It is
not an official Glade release and is not supported by the Glade developer.
Credit for the original kernel belongs to Glade and its contributors:

https://github.com/hrihq/Glade-Kernel

The branch is based on Glade commit
``2aba3f843f402cc205d5adf3f5d60825b4890e6a``. Its source-level change fixes
the ``mm/vmalloc.c`` sequence iterator: ``s_start()`` acquires
``vmap_purge_lock`` and ``vmap_area_lock``, so ``s_stop()`` now releases both
locks in reverse order.

Binary and source relationship
------------------------------

The tested recovery-flashable kernel was **not compiled from this branch**.
The existing ``4.14.357-glade-g2aba3f843f40-dirty`` ARM64 kernel Image was
patched directly: exactly 24 bytes at decompressed Image offset ``0x139458``
were replaced to add the missing ``mutex_unlock(&vmap_purge_lock)`` call, and
the boot image was then repacked. This branch records the source-equivalent
repair for review and reference; it does not claim byte-for-byte reproduction
of that binary.

Known ``-dirty`` source gap -- placeholder
------------------------------------------

The Glade developer confirmed that the tested ``-dirty`` binary was built
from commit ``2aba3f843f402cc205d5adf3f5d60825b4890e6a`` plus uncommitted local
changes. Those local changes have not yet been provided and are not present in
this branch.

**TODO:** When the developer supplies those changes, preserve and add them
with clear provenance, then document the matching build configuration and
toolchain. Until that happens, this branch must not be described as the exact
or complete corresponding source for the shipped ``-dirty`` binary. Anyone
redistributing that binary under GPLv2 must resolve this source gap rather
than relying on this branch alone.

Linux kernel
============

This file was moved to Documentation/admin-guide/README.rst

Please notice that there are several guides for kernel developers and users.
These guides can be rendered in a number of formats, like HTML and PDF.

In order to build the documentation, use ``make htmldocs`` or
``make pdfdocs``.

There are various text files in the Documentation/ subdirectory,
several of them using the Restructured Text markup notation.
See Documentation/00-INDEX for a list of what is contained in each file.

Please read the Documentation/process/changes.rst file, as it contains the
requirements for building and running the kernel, and information about
the problems which may result by upgrading your kernel.
